{
  "Small badge beside the product name; labels this page as an interactive demo/sandbox, not a children's playground\u0004playground": "playground",
  "Tooltip on a status dot. 'isReady()' is an API name — keep it verbatim. 'gate UI on this' = show the UI only once it is ready; 'flash-of-untranslated-content' is the technical term for untranslated text briefly showing (FOUC)\u0004isReady() — gate UI on this to avoid flash-of-untranslated-content": "isReady() — gate UI on this to avoid flash-of-untranslated-content",
  "ready": "ready",
  "loading…": "loading…",
  "Heading above the sidebar table-of-contents navigation links\u0004On this page": "On this page",
  "Hero tagline (marketing register). 'Angular' is the framework name and 'i18n' the abbreviation for internationalization — keep both. 'signal-first' means built around Angular Signals; render the technical sense, not 'first signal'\u0004Angular i18n, the signal-first way": "Angular i18n, the signal-first way",
  "Hero subheading (marketing prose). Keep the product/tech names verbatim: 'ng-linguo', 'Angular', 'SignalStore', 'HTML', 'i18n'. 'signals'/'store methods' are Angular Signals concepts; 'read translations'/'change the language' describe data access, not literal reading/writing\u0004ng-linguo is a complete internationalization (i18n) toolkit for Angular, built on a SignalStore. You read translations as signals, change the language through store methods, and your translators never touch a line of HTML. Use the language switcher above and watch every example on this page update instantly.": "ng-linguo is a complete internationalization (i18n) toolkit for Angular, built on a SignalStore. You read translations as signals, change the language through store methods, and your translators never touch a line of HTML. Use the language switcher above and watch every example on this page update instantly.",
  "Highlight title. Means: the English source text itself is used as the translation lookup key (no separate key names)\u0004English is the key": "English is the key",
  "Highlight body. 'lookup key' = the value used to find a translation; 'dotted key paths' = names like home.header.title used by other i18n libraries; 'AI model' = an LLM doing the translation\u0004Write real sentences in your components — that text is the lookup key. No dotted key paths to invent or keep in sync, and an AI model always sees full, meaningful context.": "Write real sentences in your components — that text is the lookup key. No dotted key paths to invent or keep in sync, and an AI model always sees full, meaningful context.",
  "Highlight title: translation files contain plain text only, no HTML markup\u0004Translators never see HTML": "Translators never see HTML",
  "Highlight body. 'Named slots' = the [name] bracket placeholders; 'Cross-site scripting' = the XSS security vulnerability\u0004Named slots bind to your own templates, so links and bold text render as real Angular while the file stays plain text. Cross-site scripting is impossible by design.": "Named slots bind to your own templates, so links and bold text render as real Angular while the file stays plain text. Cross-site scripting is impossible by design.",
  "Highlight title: correct grammatical forms (plurals, gender, etc.) in each language\u0004Real grammar, every language": "Real grammar, every language",
  "Highlight body. Keep 'ICU MessageFormat 2' verbatim (a standard). 'locale-aware numbers' = numbers formatted per language\u0004ICU MessageFormat 2 handles plurals, ordinals, gender, and locale-aware numbers — Polish gets four plural forms, English gets two, from a single message.": "ICU MessageFormat 2 handles plurals, ordinals, gender, and locale-aware numbers — Polish gets four plural forms, English gets two, from a single message.",
  "Highlight title. 'Additive' = re-running the extractor adds to your files without overwriting existing translations\u0004Additive extraction": "Additive extraction",
  "Highlight body. 'extractor' = the CLI that scans source for strings; 'catalogs' = the per-language translation files\u0004Re-run the extractor any time: new and changed strings merge into your catalogs while every existing translation is kept.": "Re-run the extractor any time: new and changed strings merge into your catalogs while every existing translation is kept.",
  "Highlight title: translating the app using an AI / LLM\u0004Translate with AI": "Translate with AI",
  "Highlight body. 'guided command-line tool' = the interactive CLI; 'chat model' = an LLM such as ChatGPT/Gemini/Claude\u0004A guided command-line tool copies a prompt for any chat model, or calls your own AI provider. Adding a whole new language is a single command.": "A guided command-line tool copies a prompt for any chat model, or calls your own AI provider. Adding a whole new language is a single command.",
  "Highlight title. Keep 'SSR' (server-side rendering) and 'tree-shakeable' (unused code is dropped from the bundle); 'Zoneless' = works without Angular's zone.js\u0004Zoneless, SSR-safe, tree-shakeable": "Zoneless, SSR-safe, tree-shakeable",
  "Highlight body. Keep 'signals' (Angular Signals) and 'zone.js' (Angular library name) verbatim; 'renders on the server' = supports server-side rendering\u0004Reactivity is signals all the way down. No zone.js, it renders on the server, and optional features ship only when you import them.": "Reactivity is signals all the way down. No zone.js, it renders on the server, and optional features ship only when you import them.",
  "Section-group label for the first group of examples: installing the library and the three basic ways to translate (the t pipe, the [t] directive, and injectTranslate)\u0004Getting started": "Getting started",
  "Section heading: initial setup / installation of the library\u0004Setup": "Setup",
  "Card title. 'translation store' = the ng-linguo SignalStore that holds translations (a noun)\u0004Set up the translation store": "Set up the translation store",
  "Card body (developer-docs tone, step-by-step). Keep API names verbatim: provideTranslate(), restoreLang(), injectTranslate(). 'persisted → browser → default' is the order restoreLang() chooses the startup language in; 'keyed by' = uses as the lookup key; 'CLI' = the command-line tool\u0004Three steps to a translated app: (1) register the store with provideTranslate() — it never fetches on its own; (2) load a language at startup with restoreLang(), which picks persisted → browser → default; (3) translate with the pipe, directive, or injectTranslate(). Translation files are plain JSON keyed by the original English text — generate them with the CLI shown under Tooling & workflow below.": "Three steps to a translated app: (1) register the store with provideTranslate() — it never fetches on its own; (2) load a language at startup with restoreLang(), which picks persisted → browser → default; (3) translate with the pipe, directive, or injectTranslate(). Translation files are plain JSON keyed by the original English text — generate them with the CLI shown under Tooling & workflow below.",
  "Section heading. 'pipe' is the Angular template-pipe concept — keep the technical term; [code] wraps the code symbol 't'\u0004The [code]t[/code] pipe": "The [code]t[/code] pipe",
  "Card title. 'template' = the Angular HTML template\u0004Translate in the template": "Translate in the template",
  "Card body. 'lookup key' = the value used to find a translation; the point is that the original English is both what you write and the key. 'never empty' = no blank UI\u0004Write the English text straight into your template — that text is the lookup key. If a translation is missing, the pipe shows the English instead, so the screen is never empty.": "Write the English text straight into your template — that text is the lookup key. If a translation is missing, the pipe shows the English instead, so the screen is never empty.",
  "Welcome back!": "Welcome back!",
  "Section heading. 'directive' is the Angular directive concept — keep the technical term; [code] wraps the code symbol '[t]'\u0004The [code][[t][/code] directive": "The [code][[t][/code] directive",
  "Card title. 'element' = an HTML / DOM element\u0004Translate an element": "Translate an element",
  "Card body. 'signals' = Angular Signals; 'translation file' = the JSON dictionary; 'directive' = the Angular [t] directive; 'RxJS subscriptions to manage' = manual subscribe/unsubscribe bookkeeping\u0004Put the text on the element itself. The directive watches the store's signals for you, so when a translation file finishes loading the element updates on its own — no RxJS subscriptions to manage.": "Put the text on the element itself. The directive watches the store's signals for you, so when a translation file finishes loading the element updates on its own — no RxJS subscriptions to manage.",
  "Card title. 'TypeScript' is the programming language name — keep verbatim\u0004Translate in TypeScript": "Translate in TypeScript",
  "Card body. 'the pipe' = the Angular t pipe; 'TypeScript' = component class code; keep computed() verbatim; 'parameters' = the ICU placeholder values; 'updates automatically' = stays reactive\u0004The pipe's equivalent for TypeScript. Call it inside a computed() and the result updates automatically whenever your parameters or the active language change.": "The pipe's equivalent for TypeScript. Call it inside a computed() and the result updates automatically whenever your parameters or the active language change.",
  "Section-group label: examples that rely on ICU MessageFormat — plurals, ordinals, gender selection, and number formatting\u0004Plurals & formatting": "Plurals & formatting",
  "Section heading: grammatical plural forms (one file / many files)\u0004Plurals": "Plurals",
  "Card title. 'Counting plurals' = cardinal forms (1 file / 2 files), as opposed to ordinals like 1st/2nd\u0004Counting plurals across languages": "Counting plurals across languages",
  "Card body. one/few/many/other are CLDR plural-category names — keep them in English; 'count' is a variable name (keep); the point is that different languages need a different number of plural forms\u0004English has two forms (one/other); Polish has four (one/few/many/other). You can also give an exact '0' form when 'No new messages' reads better than '0 messages'. Step count through 1, 2, 5, 22 in Polish to watch the form change.": "English has two forms (one/other); Polish has four (one/few/many/other). You can also give an exact '0' form when 'No new messages' reads better than '0 messages'. Step count through 1, 2, 5, 22 in Polish to watch the form change.",
  "Section heading: ordinal numbers (1st, 2nd, 3rd …), not cardinal counts\u0004Ordinals": "Ordinals",
  "Card title. 'Ordinal numbers' = ranking forms; keep the 1st/2nd/3rd examples as English ordinal suffixes\u0004Ordinal numbers (1st, 2nd, 3rd …)": "Ordinal numbers (1st, 2nd, 3rd …)",
  "Card body. st/nd/rd/th are the English ordinal suffixes; one/two/few/other are CLDR category names (keep in English); EN/PL/DE meaning English/Polish/German; '{n}.' shows the number followed by a period\u0004English picks st/nd/rd/th depending on the number (one/two/few/other); Polish and German just add a period, like '{n}.'.": "English picks st/nd/rd/th depending on the number (one/two/few/other); Polish and German just add a period, like '{n}.'.",
  "Section heading. 'select' is the ICU MessageFormat 'select' construct (pick a variant by gender) — not a dropdown and not the verb 'to select'\u0004Gender select": "Gender select",
  "Card title. This demonstrates the ICU 'select' construct: choosing a different sentence depending on a value (here, gender)\u0004Pick the wording by gender": "Pick the wording by gender",
  "Card body. 'pronoun' = she/he/they; 'verb ending' = a verb form that changes with gender (as in Polish); 'matches on count and gender at the same time' = one message that depends on two values at once\u0004English changes the pronoun (she/he/they); Polish changes the verb ending. The second line matches on count and gender at the same time — and every combination can read differently from one language to the next.": "English changes the pronoun (she/he/they); Polish changes the verb ending. The second line matches on count and gender at the same time — and every combination can read differently from one language to the next.",
  "Section heading: locale-aware number formatting (digit-group separators)\u0004Number formatting": "Number formatting",
  "Card title. 'Digit grouping' = the separators inside large numbers (1,000 vs 1.000 vs 1 000), which differ by language\u0004Digit grouping per language": "Digit grouping per language",
  "Card body. ':number' is an ICU formatter token (keep verbatim); en/de/pl meaning English/German/Polish; the figures show how each language groups thousands\u0004The :number formatter groups digits the way each language expects: en 1,234,567 · de 1.234.567 · pl 1 234 567.": "The :number formatter groups digits the way each language expects: en 1,234,567 · de 1.234.567 · pl 1 234 567.",
  "Section-group label: more advanced translation features — disambiguating identical words by context, and rich text with slot tags\u0004Advanced messages": "Advanced messages",
  "Section heading. 'Context' = the translation disambiguation feature (gettext msgctxt); 'homonyms' = words spelled the same but meaning different things\u0004Context (homonyms)": "Context (homonyms)",
  "Card title about homonyms / disambiguation\u0004Same word, different meaning": "Same word, different meaning",
  "Card body. 'context' is the ng-linguo option name (keep verbatim); 'key' = lookup key; 'msgctxt' is the gettext technical term (keep); the same English word can map to different translations\u0004A context label becomes part of the key (gettext calls it msgctxt) and also tells the translator which meaning you mean. Switch to Polish to see each one translated differently.": "A context label becomes part of the key (gettext calls it msgctxt) and also tells the translator which meaning you mean. Switch to Polish to see each one translated differently.",
